AND event_time <= '2026-10-06 11:00:00';
14. Cassandra Consistency Levels
Keyspace
CREATE KEYSPACE TestDB
WITH replication = {
'class':'SimpleStrategy',
'replication_factor':3
};
Use keyspace
USE TestDB;
Consistency ONE
CONSISTENCY ONE;
SELECT * FROM Students;

Only one replica needs to respond.

QUORUM
CONSISTENCY QUORUM;
SELECT * FROM Students;

For 3 replicas, a majority must respond.

ALL
CONSISTENCY ALL;
SELECT * FROM Students;

All replicas must respond.

Comparison
ONE    → High availability, low latency
QUORUM → Balance of consistency and availability
ALL    → Highest consistency, lower availability
15. Complete NoSQL Application – Event Logging Using Cassandra
Step 1: Create Keyspace
CREATE KEYSPACE EventDB
WITH replication = {
'class':'SimpleStrategy',
'replication_factor':3
};
USE EventDB;
Step 2: Create Table
CREATE TABLE EventLog (
    user_id text,
    event_time timestamp,
    event_type text,
    status text,
    device text,
    PRIMARY KEY (user_id,event_time)
);
Step 3: Insert Data
INSERT INTO EventLog VALUES
('U101','2026-10-06 10:00:00','LOGIN','SUCCESS','Mobile');

INSERT INTO EventLog VALUES
('U101','2026-10-06 10:10:00','VIEW','SUCCESS','Laptop');

INSERT INTO EventLog VALUES
('U102','2026-10-06 10:20:00','LOGIN','SUCCESS','Mobile');
Step 4: Query
SELECT * FROM EventLog
WHERE user_id='U101';
Step 5: Update
UPDATE EventLog
SET status='FAILED'
WHERE user_id='U101'
AND event_time='2026-10-06 10:00:00';
Step 6: Delete
DELETE FROM EventLog
WHERE user_id='U101'
AND event_time='2026-10-06 10:10:00';
Step 7: Consistency
CONSISTENCY QUORUM;

This provides a balance between consistency and availability.

Step 8: Scaling

Cassandra supports:

Horizontal scaling by adding nodes.
Partitioning using the partition key (user_id).
Replication across multiple nodes.
Fault tolerance when a node fails.
Distributed request processing.
